# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, which is a
# per-clone setting rather than a property of the repository. That is why
# `git diff` currently shows whole-file churn on files nobody edited: whether
# a checkout produces CRLF or LF depends on who cloned it. .gitattributes
# moves that decision into the repo, so every clone agrees.
#
# The rule below is "normalise text to LF in the index, let the platform
# decide in the working tree". Windows editors still see CRLF locally; the
# blobs stored in git are always LF.
* text=auto

# -- Explicitly LF, everywhere, no exceptions --------------------------------
#
# Anything that is read by a Linux kernel or a POSIX shell must be LF in the
# working tree too, not just in the index. A CRLF shebang line makes the
# kernel look for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r" and fail with a confusing
# "no such file or directory" that names a file which plainly exists. The same
# applies to files copied into the Docker image and executed there.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yaml text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml  text eol=lf

# Python is LF by convention (PEP 8 / the interpreter's own sources) and the
# package is built inside Linux containers and on Linux CI runners.
*.py            text eol=lf
*.pyi           text eol=lf

# CI and packaging metadata: consumed by Linux runners and by build backends.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.cfg           text eol=lf
*.ini           text eol=lf
*.in            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
.gitattributes  text eol=lf
.gitignore      text eol=lf
.pre-commit-config.yaml text eol=lf

# Documentation and licence text. LF keeps the sdist byte-identical whichever
# machine builds it, which matters for the reproducible-build check in CI.
*.md            text eol=lf
LICENSE         text eol=lf
py.typed        text eol=lf

# -- Explicitly CRLF ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Windows batch files are only ever run by cmd.exe. Some cmd constructs
# (notably multi-line `if`/`for` blocks and a trailing `goto :eof`) misparse
# with bare LF endings, so these keep CRLF in the working tree.
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf

# -- Binary ------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.whl           binary
*.gz            binary
*.zip           binary
*.pyc           binary

# -- Repository language stats and diff hygiene ------------------------------
# Vendored/generated content should not skew GitHub's language bar or clutter
# `git diff` output.
*.lock          -diff
skills-lock.json linguist-generated=true
